-- title:
--   Lemma 8.14 — Online-to-batch martingale bound
-- statement:
--   **Statement (Lemma 8.14, p. 202, PDF p. 219).** Let $S=((x_1,y_1),\dots,(x_T,y_T))$ be
--   drawn i.i.d. according to $D$, $L$ a loss bounded by $M$, and $h_1,\dots,h_T$ the sequence
--   of hypotheses generated by an on-line algorithm $A$ sequentially processing $S$. Then, for
--   any $\delta>0$, with probability at least $1-\delta$: $\frac1T\sum_t R(h_t) \le
--   \frac1T\sum_t L(h_t(x_t),y_t) + M\sqrt{2\log(1/\delta)/T}$. Theorem 8.15's direct
--   predecessor (an Azuma's-inequality martingale bound on `V_t = R(h_t) − L(h_t(x_t),y_t)`).
--
--   **Formalization Note.** `OnlineHypothesis A S t` is history-adapted by construction
--   (depends only on the prefix `S_0,…,S_{t-1}`, per `BRIEF.md`'s pitfall note about the
--   martingale-difference sequence's conditional-mean-zero property), not an arbitrary sequence
--   of functions. **Revision (2026-09-19).** Added `hAmeas`/`hLmeas`, guarding
--   `GeneralizationError`'s Bochner integral (`∫ p, L(h p.1) p.2 ∂D`): without a measurability
--   hypothesis on `A`'s output and on `L`, a non-measurable hypothesis at some sample prefix
--   would junk the integral to `0`, trivializing the inequality since `hLnn` already forces the
--   right-hand side `≥ 0` — with no dependence on Azuma's inequality, this lemma's actual
--   content. The history-adapted type signature guards a *different* property (conditional
--   independence, not measurability) and does not substitute for this hypothesis. Also changed
--   `hM : 0 < M` to `hM : 0 ≤ M`, matching the book's own "`L ≤ M` for some `M ≥ 0`" (p. 202) —
--   `M = 0` is an admissible instantiation the book does not exclude, and `hM` was otherwise
--   redundant given `hLnn`/`hLb`.
-- source:
--   Mohri, Rostamizadeh & Talwalkar, Foundations of Machine Learning, 2nd ed., MIT Press 2018, p. 202, Lemma 8.14 (PDF p. 219)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_FoundationsML_OnlineLearning_GeneralizationError
import Definitions.Def_FoundationsML_OnlineLearning_OnlineHypothesis

open MeasureTheory

namespace FoundationsML.OnlineLearning

/-- Lemma 8.14 (Mohri, Rostamizadeh & Talwalkar, *Foundations of Machine Learning*, 2nd ed.,
MIT Press 2018, p. 202, PDF p. 219). Let `S = ((x_1,y_1),…,(x_T,y_T))` be drawn i.i.d.
according to `D`, `L` a loss bounded by `M`, and `h_1,…,h_T` the sequence of hypotheses
generated by an on-line algorithm `A` sequentially processing `S`. Then, for any `δ > 0`, with
probability at least `1 − δ`: `(1/T)∑_t R(h_t) ≤ (1/T)∑_t L(h_t(x_t),y_t) +
M sqrt(2log(1/δ)/T)`.

**Formalization Note.** `hAmeas`/`hLmeas` guard `GeneralizationError`'s Bochner integral: without
a measurability hypothesis tying `L`/the algorithm's output to the ambient measurable structure,
an `A` producing a non-measurable hypothesis for some sample prefix would junk
`GeneralizationError` to `0` (Mathlib's convention for a non-`AEStronglyMeasurable` integrand),
trivializing the inequality since `hLnn` already forces the right-hand side `≥ 0` — with no
dependence on Azuma's inequality, the actual content of this lemma. `hM : 0 ≤ M` (not `0 < M`):
the book's standing assumption (p. 202, opening paragraph of §8.4) is `L ≤ M` "for some `M ≥ 0`",
and `M = 0` is an admissible instantiation the book does not exclude. -/
theorem online_to_batch_martingale_bound
    {X : Type*} [MeasurableSpace X] (D : Measure (X × ℝ)) [IsProbabilityMeasure D]
    (L : ℝ → ℝ → ℝ) (M : ℝ) (hM : 0 ≤ M) (hLnn : ∀ y y', 0 ≤ L y y') (hLb : ∀ y y', L y y' ≤ M)
    (hLmeas : Measurable (Function.uncurry L))
    (T : ℕ) (hT : 0 < T) (A : (n : ℕ) → (Fin n → X × ℝ) → (X → ℝ))
    (hAmeas : ∀ (n : ℕ) (S' : Fin n → X × ℝ), Measurable (A n S'))
    (δ : ℝ) (hδ : 0 < δ) :
    (1 - δ) ≤ (Measure.pi (fun _ : Fin T => D)
      {S : Fin T → X × ℝ |
        (1 / (T : ℝ)) * ∑ t : Fin T, GeneralizationError D L (OnlineHypothesis A S t) ≤
          (1 / (T : ℝ)) * ∑ t : Fin T, L (OnlineHypothesis A S t (S t).1) (S t).2 +
            M * Real.sqrt (2 * Real.log (1 / δ) / T)}).toReal := by sorry

end FoundationsML.OnlineLearning
